1. ^&^INTRODUCTION\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THIS DOCUMENT DESCRIBES THE USER SOFTWARE INTERFACE TO THE ^^ARPA\\
^NETWORK PROVIDED THROUGH THE MODIFIED ^^DEC\\SYSTEM-10 OPERATING
SYSTEM CURRENTLY IN USE AT ^CMU.
.PARAGRAPH
^COMMUNICATION THROUGH THE ^^ARPA\\ ^NETWORK IS MANAGED BY A
COLLECTION OF ROUTINES REFERRED TO AS THE ^NETWORK ^CONTROL ^PROGRAM
OR ^^NCP\\.  ^IN THIS IMPLEMENTATION, THE ^^NCP\\ IS A PART OF THE
RESIDENT OPERATING SYSTEM (MONITOR).  ^THE ^^NCP\\ IS RESPONSIBLE
FOR COMMUNICATING WITH THE ^^IMP\\S AND WITH OTHER HOSTS ON THE
NETWORK ACCORDING TO TWO STANDARD PROTOCOLS, THE ^^IMP-H\\OST
PROTOCOL AND THE ^HOST-^HOST PROTOCOL.  ^IT IS BY MEANS OF THESE
PROTOCOLS THAT HOST COMPUTER SYSTEMS OF WIDELY VARYING HARDWARE
AND SOFTWARE ARCHITECTURE CAN COMMUNICATE WITH EACH OTHER IN A
REASONABLE MANNER.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^^IMP-H\\OST PROTOCOL AND THE ^HOST-^HOST PROTOCOL ARE
DESCRIBED IN DETAIL IN THE "^^ARPANET\\ ^PROTOCOL ^HANDBOOK"
(^^NIC\\ 7104), AT LEAST ONE COPY OF WHICH IS AVAILABLE AT EACH
SITE.  ^WHILE THE DESIGN OF THE USER SOFTWARE INTERFACE IS INTENDED
TO MAKE THESE PROTOCOLS AS TRANSPARENT AS POSSIBLE TO THE USER, AN
UNDERSTANDING OF AT LEAST THE ^HOST-^HOST PROTOCOL IS HELPFUL.
.PARAGRAPH
^COMMUNICATION BETWEEN COOPERATING PROCESSES ON DIFFERENT HOSTS
IS CARRIED OUT THROUGH SOFTWARE-DEFINED PATHS KNOWN AS ^&CONNECTIONS\&.
^A SINGLE CONNECTION CAN TRANSMIT DATA IN ONLY ONE DIRECTION;  HENCE,
ACHIEVING BIDIRECTIONAL TRANSMISSION REQUIRES THE USE OF TWO CONNECTIONS.
.PARAGRAPH
^A CONNECTION MAY BE CHARACTERIZED AS A LINK BETWEEN TWO ^&SOCKETS\&,
ONE AT EACH HOST.  ^SOCKETS ARE IDENTIFIED BY 32-BIT NUMBERS KNOWN
AS ^&SOCKET NUMBERS\&.  ^THE SIGNIFICANCE OF ANY PARTICULAR SOCKET
NUMBER IS HOST-SPECIFIC, EXCEPT THAT THE LOW-ORDER BIT INDICATES
DIRECTION OF DATA TRANSFER.  ^AN EVEN SOCKET NUMBER SIGNIFIES A RECEIVE
SOCKET, WHILE AN ODD SOCKET NUMBER SIGNIFIES A TRANSMIT SOCKET.
^THUS, ANY CONNECTION MUST CONSIST OF TWO SOCKETS HAVING OPPOSITE
LOW-ORDER BITS, AN ODD SOCKET AT THE SENDING HOST AND AN EVEN SOCKET
AT THE RECEIVING HOST.
.PARAGRAPH
^ESTABLISHING A CONNECTION BETWEEN TWO HOSTS REQUIRES THE ACTIVE
COOPERATION OF THE ^^NCP\\S OF THE RESPECTIVE HOST
SYSTEMS;  THIS, IN TURN, USUALLY REQUIRES THE COOPERATION
OF A USER PROCESS AT EACH HOST, WHICH DIRECTS ITS LOCAL ^^NCP\\
TO PERFORM THE NECESSARY OPERATIONS FOR OPENING A CONNECTION
ACCORDING TO THE ^HOST-^HOST PROTOCOL.  ^ONCE OPEN, A CONNECTION MAY
BE USED FOR DATA TRANSMISSION FROM THE SENDING HOST TO THE RECEIVING
HOST, UNTIL EITHER PROCESS ELECTS TO CLOSE THE CONNECTION, AT
WHICH POINT IT CEASES TO EXIST.
.PARAGRAPH
^A FURTHER ATTRIBUTE OF A CONNECTION IS ITS ^&BYTE SIZE\&, WHICH IS
THE LOGICAL UNIT OF DATA TRANSMISSION.  ^THE BYTE SIZE IS ESTABLISHED
WHEN THE CONNECTION IS OPENED AND IS UNCHANGED FOR THE LIFE OF THE
CONNECTION.
.PARAGRAPH
^FROM THE VIEWPOINT OF A PROCESS AT A PARTICULAR HOST, THE SOCKET
NUMBERS CHARACTERIZING A CONNECTION ARE REFERRED TO AS THE
^&LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER\& AND THE ^&REMOTE SOCKET NUMBER\&.
.PARAGRAPH
^ALL NUMBERS IN THIS DOCUMENT ARE DECIMAL EXCEPT WHERE OTHERWISE
NOTED.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2. ^&^^DEC\\SYSTEM-10 ^IMPLEMENTATION\&
.PARAGRAPH
^FROM A USER'S OR USER PROGRAM'S POINT OF VIEW, AN ALREADY-ESTABLISHED
CONNECTION MAY BE TREATED EXACTLY LIKE ANY OTHER DEVICE IN THE
SYSTEM.  ^THIS PSEUDO-DEVICE HAS A PHYSICAL NAME OF ^^IMP\\N
(E.G., ^^IMP0\\ OR ^^IMP5\\) AND A PROGRAMMER-ASSIGNABLE LOGICAL
NAME.  ^AN ^^IMP\\ DEVICE IS CAPABLE OF SUPPORTING A RECEIVE CONNECTION,
MAKING IT AN INPUT-ONLY DEVICE; A TRANSMIT CONNECTION, MAKING
IT AN OUTPUT-ONLY DEVICE; OR TWO SEPARATE CONNECTIONS (ONE RECEIVE,
ONE TRANSMIT), MAKING IT A BIDIRECTIONAL DEVICE.  ^INPUT/OUTPUT
OPERATIONS (^^UUO\\S) SUCH AS ^^OPEN, INIT, IN, INPUT, OUT,\\ AND
^^OUTPUT\\ OPERATE NORMALLY.  ^^CLOSE\\  HAS ITS NORMAL DEVICE-INDEPENDENT
FUNCTION OF FORCING OUT ANY REMAINING BUFFERED DATA, BUT DOES NOT
TRANSMIT AN END-OF-FILE (THERE BEING NO SUCH CONCEPT IN THE ^HOST-^HOST
PROTOCOL ASIDE FROM CLOSING THE CONNECTION).  ^THUS, EXISTING PROGRAMS
CAN BE EXPECTED TO PERFORM ^I/^O OVER THE NETWORK WITHOUT CHANGE,
PROVIDED THAT THE NECESSARY CONNECTIONS ARE ESTABLISHED BY THE
USER OR SOME OTHER PROGRAM BEFORE INITIATING DATA TRANSFER AND
CLOSED AFTERWARD.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1 ^&^^IMPUUO\\ ^PROGRAMMING\&
.PARAGRAPH
^USER CONTROL OVER THE OPENING, MANAGEMENT, AND CLOSING OF CONNECTIONS
IS PROVIDED THROUGH A SPECIAL SUPERVISOR CALL (^^UUO\\) CALLED
THE ^^IMPUUO\\.
.PARAGRAPH
^A STANDARD SYSTEM PROGRAM (^^CUSP\\) CALLED ^^IMPCOM\\ EXISTS
FOR ALLOWING THE USER TO ISSUE COMMANDS FOR PERFORMING ALMOST
ALL ^^IMPUUO\\ OPERATIONS.  ^^IMPCOM\\ IS INVOKED BY THE
'^^IMP\\' MONITOR COMMAND.  ^DOCUMENTATION FOR ^^IMPCOM\\ IS
PROVIDED ON-LINE AND MAY BE OBTAINED BY ISSUING THE MONITOR
COMMAND '^^IMP#HELP\\'.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.1 ^&^DEFINITIONS\&
.PARAGRAPH
^IN THE FOLLOWING DESCRIPTIONS OF THE ^^IMPUUO\\ CALL, FREQUENT
REFERENCES ARE MADE TO SYMBOLS DEFINING SUCH QUANTITIES AS
BIT AND FIELD POSITIONS AND WORD OFFSETS IN BLOCKS AND TABLES.
^ALL SUCH SYMBOLS ARE DEFINED IN THE FILE ^^IMP.MAC\\,
WHICH IS KEPT ON ^^SYS:\\.  ^ITS COMPILED FORM IS ^^IMP.UNV\\,
WHICH IS KEPT ON ^^UNV:\\.  ^IT IS A UNIVERSAL FILE, AND MAY BE
ACCESSED FROM WITHIN A ^^MACRO-10\\ PROGRAM VIA THE STATEMENT
.NOFILL
.TAB STOPS 8
.SKIP 1
^^	SEARCH IMP\\
.SKIP 1
.FILL
^IT IS RECOMMENDED THAT ALL USER PROGRAMS BE CODED TO UTILIZE
THIS DEFINITION FILE SO AS TO AVOID ANY PROBLEMS THAT MIGHT ARISE
SHOULD CERTAIN OF THE DEFINITIONS BE CHANGED AT ANY TIME
IN THE FUTURE.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.2 ^&^CALLING ^SEQUENCE\&
.PARAGRAPH
^ALL ^^IMPUUO\\ CALLS ARE MADE USING THE FOLLOWING FORMAT:
.NOFILL
.SKIP 1
.TAB STOPS 8,16
^^	MOVE	AC,[\\^CONTROL WORD]
^^	CALL	AC,[SIXBIT /IMPUUO/]
	  E\\RROR RETURN
	^NORMAL RETURN
.FILL
.SKIP 1
^THE CORRESPONDING ^^CALLI UUO\\ INDEX IS -5 AT ^HARVARD AND
-17 (OCTAL) AT ^CARNEGIE-^MELLON;  HENCE, FOR REASONS OF SOFTWARE
TRANSPORTABILITY, USER PROGRAMS SHOULD USE ONLY THE ^^CALL\\ AND
NOT THE ^^CALLI\\.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FORMAT OF THE CONTROL WORD IS AS FOLLOWS:
.NOFILL
.SKIP 1
^&^BITS	^SYMBOL	^SIGNIFICANCE\&
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.SKIP 1
.FILL
.INDENT -16
##0	^^IF.NWT\\	^IF SET, THE ^^IMPUUO\\ WILL NOT WAIT FOR
COMPLETION OF THE SPECIFIED FUNCTION, EVEN IF A RESPONSE FROM
THE REMOTE HOST IS REQUIRED.  ^IT IS THE USER'S RESPONSIBILITY
TO CHECK FOR COMPLETION OF THE FUNCTION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##0	^^IF.IEC\\	^THIS BIT HAS MEANING ONLY IN THE ^^.IUXTT\\
FUNCTION, WHERE ^^IF.NWT\\ DOES NOT APPLY.
^SEE ^^.IUXTT\\ (BELOW) FOR AN EXPLANATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##1	^^IF.PRV\\	^IF SET, AND THE USER HAS APPROPRIATE
SPECIAL PRIVILEGES, THE FUNCTION WILL BE ALLOWED EVEN IF THE
SPECIFIED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE BELONGS TO ANOTHER JOB.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##2	^^IF.ALS\\	^IF SET, SPECIFIES THAT THE LOCAL
SOCKET NUMBER GIVEN IN ^^.IBLCL\\ (SEE BELOW) IS ABSOLUTE RATHER
THAN USER- OR JOB-RELATIVE.  ^EXCEPT IN SPECIAL CIRCUMSTANCES,
THIS REQUIRES SPECIAL PRIVILEGES AND THE ^^IF.PRV\\ BIT SET.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##3	^^IF.NEW\\	^IF SET, SPECIFIES THAT THE PARAMETER
BLOCK IS IN THE NEW FORMAT, AS DESCRIBED BELOW.  ^ALL PROGRAMS
SHOULD SET THIS BIT AND USE THE NEW FORMAT.  ^THE OLD FORMAT
IS DESCRIBED IN THE ^APPENDIX FOR THE REFERENCE WHEN UPDATING
OLD PROGRAMS TO THE NEW FORMAT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
8-10	^^IF.TIM\\	^A 3-BIT CODE SPECIFYING THE TIMEOUT
PERIOD FOR THE REQUESTED FUNCTION.  ^IF ZERO, THE TIMEOUT IS SET TO A DEFAULT
OF 30 SECONDS.  ^IF NONZERO, THE TIMEOUT INTERVAL IS 4#*#2_^N
SECONDS, WHERE N IS THE CONTENTS OF THE ^^IF.TIM\\ FIELD.  ^THUS,
THE USER MAY CHOOSE A TIMEOUT INTERVAL FROM 8 SECONDS TO OVER 8
MINUTES.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
11-17	^^IF.FNC\\	^A 7-BIT CODE SPECIFYING THE ^^NCP\\ FUNCTION
TO BE EXECUTED.  ^THE AVAILABLE FUNCTIONS ARE DESCRIBED IN ^SECTION
2.1.3.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
18-35		^THE ADDRESS OF A DATA BLOCK, FOR THOSE FUNCTIONS
THAT REQUIRE ONE.  ^THE DATA BLOCK MUST LIE WITHIN THE USER PROGRAM'S
LOW SEGMENT OR ACCUMULATORS.  ^THE DATA BLOCK FORMAT IS DESCRIBED
BELOW AND IN THE DESCRIPTIONS OF THE INDIVIDUAL FUNCTIONS.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^MANY FUNCTIONS REQUIRE A STANDARD PARAMETER BLOCK, CALLED A ^&CONNECTION
BLOCK\&, WHOSE FORMAT IS AS FOLLOWS:
.SKIP 1
.NOFILL
^&^OFFSET	^SYMBOL	^CONTENTS\&
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.SKIP 1
.FILL
.INDENT -16
##0	^^.IBDEV\\	^THE ^^SIXBIT\\ PHYSICAL OR LOGICAL ^^IMP\\
DEVICE NAME FOR THE CONNECTION BEING ACCESSED.  ^IF THIS FIELD IS
BLANK, CONTAINS THE GENERIC DEVICE NAME ^^'IMP'\\, OR CONTAINS
A NAME NOT YET CORRESPONDING TO ANY LOGICAL OR PHYSICAL DEVICE
NAME, AND THE FUNCTION IS ONE OF A SMALL SET THAT PERMIT IMPLICIT
DEVICE ASSIGNMENT (^^.IUCON, .IULSN, .IUREQ\\), THEN AN UNUSED
^^IMP\\ DEVICE WILL BE ALLOCATED AND ASSIGNED TO THE USER.
^THE SUPPLIED NAME (IF ANY) WILL BE MADE THE LOGICAL NAME OF THE
DEVICE, AND THE PHYSICAL NAME WILL BE RETURNED IN THE ^^.IBDEV\\
WORD OF THE CONNECTION BLOCK.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##1	^^.IBSTT\\	^IN THE ^^.IUSTT\\ FUNCTION,
THE OWNING JOB NUMBER, STATUS BITS, AND CONNECTION STATE ARE
RETURNED IN THIS WORD.
.INDENT -16
	^^.IBERR\\	^ON AN ERROR RETURN FROM THE ^^IMPUUO\\
FOR ANY FUNCTION EXCEPT ^^.IUXIS\\, AN ERROR CODE IS DEPOSITED
RIGHT-JUSTIFIED IN THIS WORD.  ^THE ERROR CODES ARE DESCRIBED IN
^SECTION 2.1.4.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##2	^^.IBLCL\\	^THE LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER FOR THE CONNECTION.
^WHEN SUPPLIED AS AN ARGUMENT BY THE PROGRAM, ONLY THE LOW-ORDER
NINE BITS ARE USED, UNLESS THE ^^IF.ALS\\ BIT IS SET
IN THE ^^IMPUUO\\ CONTROL WORD.  ^THE ^^NCP\\ CONSTRUCTS A FULL 32-BIT LOCAL
SOCKET NUMBER BY APPENDING THESE BITS TO EITHER THE USER'S PROGRAMMER
NUMBER (IF THE SUPPLIED LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER IS BETWEEN 0 AND 255)
OR THE USER'S JOB NUMBER (IF THE SUPPLED NUMBER IS FROM 256 TO 511).
^HOWEVER, IF THE SIGN BIT OF THE ^^.IBLCL\\ WORD IS SET,
THE MONITOR FILLS ALL BUT THE LOW-ORDER EIGHT BITS WITH A GUARANTEED
UNIQUE NUMBER.  ^SEE ^SECTION 2.1.7 FOR FURTHER DETAILS.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##3	^^.IBHST\\	^THE FOREIGN ^SITE NUMBER (^NETWORK ADDRESS),
WHICH HAS 3 PARTS: BITS 4-11 CONTAIN THE ^NETWORK NUMBER, 12-19
CONTAIN THE ^HOST NUMBER OF THE MACHINE, AND 20-35 CONTAIN THE ^^IMP\\
NUMBER OF THE SITE.  ^SINCE INTER-NETWORK ADDRESSING IS NOT YET
SUPPORTED BY ^^ARPA\\, THE NETWORK NUMBER IS CURRENTLY IGNORED, AND
RETURNED AS 0'S.  ^THIS WILL NOT BE TRUE IN THE NEAR FUTURE, SO
PROGRAMS SHOULD BE WRITTEN TO LEAVE THIS FIELD OF THE ^SITE NUMBER
BLANK.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##4	^^.IBRMT\\	^THE REMOTE SOCKET NUMBER, WHICH IDENTIFIES
THE PROCESS CONNECTION AT THE FOREIGN HOST.  ^UNLIKE THE LOCAL
SOCKET NUMBER, THE USER PROGRAM MUST SUPPLY ALL 32 BITS (RIGHT
JUSTIFIED).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##5	^^.IBBYT\\	^THE CONNECTION BYTE SIZE, BETWEEN 1 AND 255,
IN THE LEFT HALF OF THE WORD.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SKIP 1
^THE SYMBOL ^^.IBSIZ\\ (VALUE 6) DENOTES THE CURRENT SIZE OF THE
STANDARD CONNECTION BLOCK.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.3 ^&^FUNCTION ^CODES\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FOLLOWING ARE THE ^^IMPUUO\\ FUNCTION CODES CURRENTLY ASSIGNED.
^THESE CODES ARE PLACED BY THE PROGRAMMER IN THE ^^IF.FNC\\
FIELD OF THE CONTROL WORD PASSED IN THE ACCUMULATOR REFERENCED BY
THE ^^AC\\ FIELD OF THE ^^IMPUUO\\.  ^EXCEPT AS OTHERWISE INDICATED,
FUNCTION CODES 0 THROUGH 63 REQUIRE NETWORK ACCESS PRIVILEGES, AND
FUNCTION CODES 64 THROUGH 127 REQUIRE SPECIAL ^^NCP\\ CONTROL PRIVILEGES.
^THESE PRIVILEGES ARE DENOTED BY THE SYMBOLS ^^JP.NET\\ AND ^^JP.IMP\\
RESPECTIVELY (THEIR ACTUAL VALUE IS SITE-DEPENDENT), AND ARE ASSIGNED
BY THE ESTABLISHMENT ON A PER-USER BASIS.
.SKIP 1
.NOFILL
^&^CODE	^SYMBOL	^FUNCTION\&
.SKIP 1
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.FILL
.INDENT -16
##0	^^.IUSTT\\	^RETURNS THE STATUS OF A PARTICULAR CONNECTION.
^THIS IS AN UNPRIVILEGED OPERATION.  ^GIVEN THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE NAME
(PHYSICAL OR LOGICAL) AND THE LOW-ORDER BIT OF THE LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER
(TO INDICATE THE INPUT OR OUTPUT CONNECTION ASSOCIATED WITH THE
PARTICULAR ^^IMP\\ DEVICE), THE ^^NCP\\ RETURNS INFORMATION ABOUT
THAT CONNECTION IN ALL FIELDS OF THE CONNECTION BLOCK.  ^^.IBDEV\\
GETS THE LOGICAL DEVICE NAME IF THERE IS ONE, ELSE THE PHYSICAL
DEVICE NAME.  ^^.IBSTT\\ GETS THE OWNER JOB NUMBER IN THE LEFT HALF
(0 IF THE DEVICE IS UNASSIGNED) AND THE STATUS BITS AND CONNECTION
STATE IN THE RIGHT HALF (SEE ^SECTION 2.1.5).  ^^.IBLCL\\
GETS THE FULL LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER (32 BITS), ^^.IBHST\\ GETS THE
FOREIGN SITE NUMBER,
^^.IBRMT\\ GETS THE REMOTE SOCKET NUMBER (32 BITS), AND ^^.IBBYT\\.
GETS THE CONNECTION BYTE SIZE IN ITS LEFT HALF.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##3	^^.IUCON\\	^INITIATES OR ACCEPTS A CONNECTION BETWEEN
THE SPECIFIED LOCAL AND REMOTE SOCKETS.  ^THE FOREIGN HOST, BYTE SIZE,
AND BOTH SOCKET NUMBERS MUST BE SPECIFIED IN THE CONNECTION BLOCK.
^UNLESS THE ^^IF.NWT\\ BIT IS SET IN THE CONTROL WORD, THE USER'S
JOB IS PLACED INTO ^I/^O WAIT UNTIL THE CONNECTION IS
COMPLETED OR AN ERROR OCCURS.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##4	^^.IUCLS\\	^BREAKS THE CONNECTION IDENTIFIED BY THE
SPECIFIED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE NAME AND THE LOW-ORDER BIT OF THE
LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER (TO INDICATE WHETHER IT IS THE RECEIVE
CONNECTION OR THE TRANSMIT CONNECTION THAT IS BEING CLOSED).
^UNLESS THE ^^IF.NWT\\ BIT IS SET IN THE CONTROL WORD, THE JOB
WAITS UNTIL THE CONNECTION IS CLOSED OR AN ERROR OCCURS.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##5	^^.IULSN\\	^SETS A LOCAL SOCKET INTO A "LISTENING"
STATE WHEREBY INCOMING REQUESTS FOR CONNECTION TO THAT SOCKET
WILL BE QUEUED BY THE ^^NCP\\ FOR SUBSEQUENT EXAMINATION AND
ACCEPTANCE OR REJECTION BY THE USER PROGRAM (BY ISSUING ^^.IUCON\\
OR ^^.IUCLS\\ FUNCTIONS, RESPECTIVELY).  ^AT PRESENT, THE LENGTH
OF THIS QUEUE IS 1.  ^THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE NAME AND
LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER MUST BE SPECIFIED IN THE CONNECTION BLOCK.
^OTHER PARAMETERS ARE OPTIONAL AND SHOULD BE SET TO ZERO IF NOT
BEING SUPPLIED.  ^INCOMING CONNECTION REQUESTS TO THIS
SOCKET NOT MATCHING SUPPLIED PARAMETERS WILL AUTOMATICALLY BE
REJECTED BY THE ^^NCP\\, AS WILL REQUESTS TO SOCKETS NOT
IN A LISTENING STATE.  ^THE ^^.IULSN\\ FUNCTION ALWAYS RETURNS
IMMEDIATELY (I.E., THE USER PROGRAM DOES NOT WAIT).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##6	^^.IUREQ\\	^IF THE SPECIFIED SOCKET IS NOT ALREADY
IN A LISTENING STATE, THE EQUIVALENT OF THE ^^.IULSN\\ FUNCTION
IS PERFORMED.  ^^.IUREQ\\ THEN WAITS UNTIL A CORRECTLY MATCHING
CONNECTION REQUEST IS RECEIVED ON THE SOCKET, AT WHICH POINT
THE BYTE SIZE, FOREIGN HOST NUMBER, AND REMOTE SOCKET NUMBER ARE
PLACED IN THE CONNECTION BLOCK AND CONTROL IS RETURNED TO THE
USER PROGRAM.  ^AT THIS POINT, THE PROGRAM MAY EITHER ACCEPT OR
REJECT THE CONNECTION BY ISSUING ^^.IUCON\\ OR ^^.IUCLS\\ FUNCTIONS,
RESPECTIVELY.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##7	^^.IUXTT\\	^CROSS-PATCHES THE USER'S TERMINAL TO
THE SPECIFIED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE, WHICH MUST HAVE BOTH INPUT AND
OUTPUT CONNECTIONS OPEN TO THE SAME HOST, AND WITH A BYTE SIZE OF 8.
^ONLY THE ^^.IBDEV\\ WORD NEED BE SUPPLIED IN THE PARAMETER BLOCK.
^ALL SUBSEQUENT CHARACTERS TYPED BY THE USER ARE TRANSMITTED OVER
THE SEND CONNECTION TO THE REMOTE HOST, AND ALL DATA RECEIVED OVER
THE RECEIVE CONNECTION IS TYPED ON THE TERMINAL.  ^THE CROSS-PATCHED
STATE IS BROKEN WHEN THE CONNECTION BECOMES CLOSED OR THE USER TYPES
THE LOCAL ESCAPE CHARACTER (INITIALLY _^__ OR CONTROL-SHIFT-^O).
^IT IS BY THIS MEANS THAT THE USER ^TELNET IS IMPLEMENTED.
^IF ^^IF.IEC\\ IS SET IN THE CONTROL WORD, THEN THE USER ^TELNET
WILL REFUSE ALL ATTEMPTS FROM THE SERVER TO ECHO.
^THIS IS THE BIT SET BY ^^/ECHO\\ IN ^^IMPCOM\\.
^THE PROTOCOL DOES NOT ALLOW THE USER END TO REFUSE TO ECHO, ONLY TO
REFUSE NOT TO ECHO, HENCE THERE IS NO CORRESPONDING BIT FOR NOT ECHOING.
^COMPLETE INFORMATION MAY BE FOUND IN THE ^^IMPCOM\\ DOCUMENTATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##9	^^.IUXNT\\	^SENDS AN INTERRUPT OVER THE SPECIFIED (INPUT
OR OUTPUT) SOCKET CONNECTION.  ^THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE NAME AND THE
LOW-ORDER BIT OF THE LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER MUST BE SPECIFIED TO
IDENTIFY THE SOCKET.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##10	^^.IUANT\\	^THE CONTENTS OF THE RIGHT HALF OF ^^.IBSTT\\
SPECIFY AN ADDRESS FOR DISPATCH UPON RECEIPT OF AN INTERRUPT ON
EITHER OF THE SOCKETS IDENTIFIED BY THE SPECIFIED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE
NAME.  ^THIS FUNCTION IS ^&NOT YET IMPLEMENTED\&.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##11	^^.IUVRS\\	^RETURNS THE VERSION NUMBERS OF THE
^^IMP\\ DEVICE SERVICE ROUTINES AND THE ^^NCP\\ IN THE LEFT AND
RIGHT HALVES, RESPECTIVELY, OF THE ^^.IBSTT\\ WORD OF THE PARAMETER
BLOCK.  ^NO ARGUMENTS NEED BE SUPPLIED BY THE USER PROGRAM.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##12	^^.IUDEA\\	^DEASSIGNS THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE SPECIFIED BY
THE CONTENTS OF ^^.IBDEV\\.  ^THIS FUNCTION IS EQUIVALENT TO THE
MONITOR-LEVEL ^^DEASSIGN\\ COMMAND AND IS INCLUDED ONLY FOR HISTORICAL
REASONS (AN ^^IMP\\ DEVICE IS NOW AUTOMATICALLY DEASSIGNED WHEN
BOTH INPUT AND OUTPUT CONNECTIONS ARE CLOSED).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##13	^^.IULHS\\	^RETURNS INFORMATION ABOUT THE ^^IMP\\
SERVICE ON THE LOCAL HOST.  ^BITS 1-8 OF THE ^^.IBDEV\\ WORD CONTAIN
THE TOTAL NUMBER OF NETWORK TERMINALS IMPLEMENTED IN THE SYSTEM.
^BITS 9-17 OF THE ^^.IBDEV\\ WORD CONTAIN THE TOTAL NUMBER OF ^^IMP\\
DEVICES.  ^BITS 18-35 OF THE ^^.IBDEV\\ WORD CONTAIN THE TERMINAL
NUMBER OF THE FIRST NETWORK TERMINAL.
^BIT 0 OF THE ^^.IBSTT\\ WORD IS SET IF THE NETWORK IS
UNAVAILABLE (BECAUSE THE ^^NCP\\ IS DISABLED OR THE ^^IMP\\ IS DOWN).
^THE SITE NUMBER IS RETURNED IN THE ^^.IBHST\\ WORD.  ^NO ARGUMENTS
NEED BE SUPPLIED BY THE USER PROGRAM, BUT OTHER FIELDS IN THE
CONNECTION BLOCK ARE RESERVED FOR FUTURE USE IN RETURNING OTHER
PARAMETERS.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##15	^^.IUGVB\\	^SENDS A ^^GVB\\ (^GIVE-^BACK) ^^NCP\\
MESSAGE OVER THE SPECIFIED CONNECTION (IDENTIFIED BY THE ^^IMP\\
DEVICE NAME AND THE LOW-ORDER BIT OF THE LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER).
^THIS FUNCTION REQUIRES SPECIAL ^^NCP\\ CONTROL PRIVILEGES
(^^JP.IMP\\) AND IS ^&NOT PRESENTLY IMPLEMENTED\&.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##16	^^.IUITY\\	^TRANSLATES BETWEEN AN ^^IMP\\ DEVICE
AND A CONNECTED TERMINAL.  ^^IMP\\S AND TERMINALS CAN HAVE ONE OF TWO
RELATIONS:  THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE CONTROLS A NETWORK TERMINAL (SERVER
^TELNET), OR THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE IS CROSS-PATCHED TO A LOCAL TERMINAL
(USER ^TELNET).  ^IF THE ^^.IBDEV\\ PARAMETER IS SUPPLIED, THE
LINE NUMBER OF ANY CONNECTED TERMINAL IS RETURNED IN
THE RIGHT HALF OF ^^.IBSTT\\;  BIT 0 IS SET IF THIS IS A NETWORK
TERMINAL AND CLEAR IF IT IS A LOCAL TERMINAL CROSS-PATCHED TO AN
^^IMP\\.  ^IF ^^.IBDEV\\ IS CLEAR, THEN THE USER PROGRAM MUST SUPPLY
A TERMINAL NUMBER IN ^^.IBSTT\\ AND SET BIT 0 TO ZERO OR 1 DEPENDING
ON WHETHER A CROSS-PATCHED OR CONTROLLING ^^IMP\\ IS DESIRED.
^THE PHYSICAL NAME OF THE CONNECTED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE IS THEN RETURNED
IN ^^.IBDEV\\.
^THIS FUNCTION IS UNPRIVILEGED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##17	^^.IUXWT\\	^THIS FUNCTION, WHICH SHOULD BE CALLED
ONLY AFTER A SUCCESSFUL RETURN FROM THE ^^.IUXTT\\ FUNCTION,
PLACES THE JOB INTO ^I/^O WAIT UNTIL THE CROSS-PATCHED STATE
IS BROKEN.  ^ONLY THE ^^.IBDEV\\ PARAMETER NEED BE SUPPLIED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##18	^^.IUPES\\	^SETS THE VARIOUS QUOTE AND ESCAPE CHARACTERS
FOR THE TERMINAL CONTROLLING THIS JOB.  ^THE PARAMETER BLOCK MUST
CONSIST OF FOUR WORDS, EACH CONTAINING ONE ^^ASCII\\ CHARACTER,
RIGHT-JUSTIFIED, SPECIFYING, RESPECTIVELY, THE QUOTE, SHIFT,
LOCAL ESCAPE, AND NETWORK ESCAPE CHARACTERS TO BE SUBSEQUENTLY
USED ON THAT TERMINAL.  ^THE MEANING AND USE OF THESE CHARACTERS
IS DESCRIBED IN THE ^^IMPCOM\\ DOCUMENTATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##19	^^.IURES\\	^READS THE CURRENT
QUOTE AND ESCAPE CHARACTERS INTO THE PARAMETER BLOCK,IN
THE FORM GIVEN IN THE DESCRIPTION OF THE ^^.IUPES\\ FUNCTION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##20	^^.IUPCP\\	^SETS A CONNECTION PARAMETER WORD
SUPPLIED IN ^^.IBSTT\\ INTO THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE SPECIFIED BY
^^.IBDEV\\.  ^THE SIGNIFICANCE OF THE DATA IN THIS WORD IS LEFT
ENTIRELY UP TO THE USER PROGRAM (BITS 0 AND 1 ARE PRESENTLY USED
BY ^^IMPCOM\\ TO REMEMBER USER ^TELNET PARAMETERS FOR EACH OPEN
CONNECTION).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##21	^^.IURCP\\	^READS THE CONNECTION PARAMETER WORD FROM
THE DEVICE SPECIFIED BY ^^.IBDEV\\ INTO ^^.IBSTT\\.  ^IF NO
^^.IUPCP\\ OPERATION HAS BEEN PERFORMED ON THE DEVICE SINCE IT WAS
FIRST ASSIGNED AND A CONNECTION OPENED, ZERO IS RETURNED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##22	^^.IUXIS\\	^RETURNS EXTENDED STATUS FOR A
SPECIFIED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE.  ^THIS FUNCTION USES A NON-STANDARD
VARIABLE LENGTH PARAMETER BLOCK DESCRIBED IN ^SECTION 2.1.6.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##23	^^.IUTRC\\	^TURNS ON AND OFF TRACE MODE FOR THE
OUTPUT CONNECTION SPECIFIED BY ^^.IBDEV\\ AND THE LOW-ORDER BIT
OF ^^.IBLCL\\.  ^IF ^^.IBSTT\\ IS NONZERO, TRACE MODE IS ENABLED;
IF ZERO, DISABLED.  ^WHEN TRACE MODE IS ENABLED, THE ^HOST-^^IMP\\
TRACE BIT IS SET ON ALL DATA MESSAGES TRANSMITTED THROUGH
THE SPECIFIED CONNECTION.  ^THIS FACILITY IS USED FOR NETWORK
MEASUREMENT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##24	^^.IUIAL\\	^SPECIFIES THE DESIRED ALLOCATION TO BE
MAINTAINED BY THE ^^NCP\\ FOR THE INPUT CONNECTION
SPECIFIED BY ^^.IBDEV\\ AND THE LOW-ORDER BIT OF ^^.IBLCL\\.
^THE PROGRAM MUST SUPPLY THE MESSAGE AND BIT ALLOCATIONS IN
^^.IBHST\\ AND ^^.IBRMT\\ RESPECTIVELY.  ^AT PRESENT, THE
HIGHEST ALLOCATIONS PERMITTED ARE 20 MESSAGES AND 16000 BITS.
^NO ERROR WILL RESULT FROM ATTEMPTING TO SPECIFY HIGHER ALLOCATIONS.
^THE PRINCIPAL USE OF THIS FUNCTION IS TO RAISE THE BIT ALLOCATION
FOR ^TELNET CONNECTIONS, WHICH IS DELIBERATELY SET VERY LOW
TO AVOID MASSIVE AMOUNTS OF TERMINAL BUFFERING.  ^FOR HIGH-SPEED
TERMINALS, IT IS DESIRABLE TO RAISE THE ALLOCATION TO AVOID PAUSES
IN OUTPUT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##64	^^.IUNOP\\	^SENDS A ^^NOP\\ (NO-OP) TO THE HOST
SPECIFIED BY ^^.IBHST\\.  ^PRIVILEGED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##65	^^.IURST\\	^SENDS A ^^RST\\ (RESET) TO THE HOST SPECIFIED
BY ^^.IBHST\\.  ^PRIVILEGED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##66	^^.IUALL\\	^SENDS OR SIMULATES RECEIPT OF AN ^^ALL\\
(ALLOCATE) MESSAGE FOR THE CONNECTION SPECIFIED BY ^^.IBDEV\\
AND THE LOW-ORDER BIT OF ^^.IBLCL\\.  ^THE PROGRAM MUST SUPPLY
THE MESSAGE AND BIT ALLOCATIONS IN ^^.IBHST\\ AND ^^.IBRMT\\
RESPECTIVELY.  ^PRIVILEGED.  ^&^NOT PRESENTLY IMPLEMENTED\&.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##69	^^.IUECO\\	^SENDS AN ^^ECO\\ (ECHO) TO THE HOST SPECIFIED
BY ^^.IBHST\\.  ^PRIVILEGED.  ^&^NOT PRESENTLY IMPLEMENTED\&.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##70	^^.IUINI\\	^REINITIALIZES THE ^^NCP\\, CLEARING ALL
CONNECTIONS AND INTERNAL TABLES, BUT DOES NOT RESTART IT.  ^PRIVILEGED.
^NO PARAMETER BLOCK REQUIRED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##71	^^.IUDWN\\	^BRINGS DOWN THE ^^NCP\\, SUCH THAT NO FURTHER
MESSAGES WILL BE SENT TO OR RECEIVED FROM THE NETWORK, BUT DOES NOT
REINITIALIZE IT.  ^THIS FUNCTION DOES NOT TAKE EFFECT
FOR ABOUT 30 SECONDS (HOWEVER, THE JOB IS NOT PUT INTO ^I/^O WAIT
FOR THIS PERIOD).  ^PRIVILEGED.  ^NO PARAMETER BLOCK REQUIRED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##72	^^.IUUP\\	^BRINGS UP THE ^^NCP\\, WITHOUT PERFORMING
ANY INITIALIZATION.  ^PRIVILEGED.  ^NO PARAMETER BLOCK REQUIRED.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.4 ^&^ERROR ^CODES\&
.PARAGRAPH
^ON AN ERROR RETURN FROM THE ^^IMPUUO\\, AN ERROR CODE IS DEPOSITED
IN THE ^^.IBERR\\ WORD OF THE PARAMETER BLOCK FOR ALL FUNCTIONS
EXCEPT ^^.IUXIS\\ (FOR ^^.IUXIS\\, THE ERROR IS DEPOSITED IN ^^.XSIST\\).
^THESE CODES HAVE THE FOLLOWING MEANING:
.SKIP 1
.NOFILL
^&^CODE	^SYMBOL	^MEANING\&
.FILL
.SKIP 1
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.INDENT -16
(UNCHANGED)	^THE ^^IMPUUO\\ IS UNIMPLEMENTED (PRESUMABLY
BECAUSE A NON-^^IMP\\ MONITOR IS BEING RUN).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##0	^^.IEILU\\	^THE REQUESTED FUNCTION IS ILLEGAL OR
UNIMPLEMENTED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##1	^^.IENSD\\	^THERE IS NO SUCH PHYSICAL OR LOGICAL
DEVICE AS THE ONE GIVEN IN ^^.IBDEV\\.  ^IF THIS ERROR OCCURS
DURING A ^^.IUCON, .IULSN\\, OR ^^.IUREQ\\ FUNCTION (WHICH PERFORM
IMPLICIT LOGICAL DEVICE ASSIGNMENT IF THE SUPPLIED NAME DOES NOT
CORRESPOND TO ANY EXISTING DEVICE), IT MEANS THAT THERE ARE NO
FREE ^^IMP\\ DEVICES AVAILABLE FOR ASSIGNMENT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##2	^^.IEDNA\\	^DEVICE NOT AVAILABLE TO THE PROGRAM
(BECAUSE IT BELONGS TO ANOTHER JOB).  ^THIS ERROR WILL OCCUR
FOR ANY NONPRIVILEGED PROGRAM ATTEMPTING TO ISSUE OPERATIONS
BESIDES ^^.IUSTT, .IUITY\\, AND ^^.IUXIS\\ TO AN ^^IMP\\ DEVICE
BELONGING TO ANOTHER JOB.  ^A PRIVILEGED PROGRAM MUST SET THE
^^IF.PRV\\ BIT TO PERFORM SUCH OPERATIONS.  ^THIS ERROR WILL
ALSO OCCUR IF A NON-PRIVILEGED PROGRAM ATTEMPTS TO CLOSE AN ^^IMP\\
DEVICE CONTROLLING A NETWORK TERMINAL (EVEN THOUGH IT BELONGS
TO THE JOB).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##3	^^.IELNU\\	^THE LOGICAL NAME SPECIFIED IS ALREADY ASSIGNED
TO A NON-^^IMP\\ DEVICE.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##4	^^.IESTT\\	^THE SPECIFIED CONNECTION IS IN AN IMPROPER
STATE FOR THE FUNCTION REQUESTED (E.G., ATTEMPTING TO OPEN A
CONNECTION THAT IS ALREADY OPEN).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##5	^^.IESOF\\	^SOCKET OPEN FAILURE (USUALLY, CONNECTION
REFUSED BY THE FOREIGN HOST BECAUSE OF INSUFFICIENT RESOURCES,
NO LISTENING PROCESS FOR THE REMOTE SOCKET, OR BYTE SIZE MISMATCH).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##6	^^.IESCF\\	^SOCKET CLOSE FAILURE.  ^THIS IS A SYSTEM
ERROR AND SHOULD NOT OCCUR.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##7	^^.IEABT\\	^INCOMING CONNECTION ABORTED.  ^A REQUEST
FOR CONNECTION WAS RECEIVED ON A LISTENING SOCKET BUT WAS THEN
ABORTED BY THE FOREIGN HOST'S CANCELLING THE REQUEST.  ^THIS ERROR
WILL OCCUR ONLY ON A ^^.IUCON\\ OPERATION TO A SOCKET IN THE
^^ABORT\\ STATE.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##8	^^.IEREQ\\	^CONNECTION DOESN'T MATCH REQUEST.  ^ONE OR
MORE PARAMETERS IN THE CONNECTION BLOCK DO NOT MATCH PARAMETERS
PREVIOUSLY SUPPLIED FOR THE SPECIFIED CONNECTION OR RECEIVED VIA
A CONNECTION REQUEST FROM THE FOREIGN HOST.  ^THIS ERROR CAN OCCUR
ON ^^.IUCON, .IULSN\\, AND ^^.IUREQ\\ FUNCTIONS.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##9	^^.IESKT\\	^SPECIFIED LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER IS
ALREADY IN USE (IN A CONNECTION THROUGH ANOTHER ^^IMP\\ DEVICE
BELONGING TO THE SAME OR A DIFFERENT JOB).  ^THIS ERROR CAN
OCCUR ON ^^.IUCON, .IULSN\\, AND ^^.IUREQ\\ FUNCTIONS.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##10	^^.IEHST\\	^ILLEGAL HOST NUMBER (HOST NUMBER WAS ZERO).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##11	^^.IEDWN\\	^THE REMOTE HOST OR ^^IMP\\ IS DOWN OR
NONEXISTENT.  ^^.IUCON\\ AND ^^.IUCLS\\ FUNCTIONS ONLY.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##12	^^.IEADR\\	^CONNECTION BLOCK ADDRESS CHECK.  ^THE
PARAMETER BLOCK DOES NOT LIE ENTIRELY WITHIN THE USER'S LOW SEGMENT
OR ACCUMULATORS.  (^OF COURSE, A MONITOR ADDRESS CHECK WILL OCCUR
IF THE ^^.IBERR\\ WORD IS OUT OF BOUNDS).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##13	^^.IETIM\\	^TIMEOUT.  ^THE REQUESTED FUNCTION WAS NOT
COMPLETED WITHIN THE SPECIFIED INTERVAL.
.TEST PAGE 4
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##14	^^.IEPAR\\	^PARAMETER SPECIFICATION ERROR.  ^AN ILLEGAL
PARAMETER WAS SUPPLIED (CAN OCCUR IN ALMOST ANY FUNCTION).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##15	^^.IENCI\\	^TERMINAL NOT CONNECTED TO ^^IMP\\.
^OCCURS IF THE ^^IMP\\ OR TERMINAL SPECIFIED IN ^^.IUITY\\
DOES NOT HAVE A TERMINAL-^^IMP\\ CONNECTION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##16	^^.IEQUO\\	^ILLEGAL QUOTE OR ESCAPE CHARACTER (^^.IUPES\\
ONLY).  ^ONE OR MORE SUPPLIED QUOTE AND ESCAPE CHARACTERS ARE EITHER
ILLEGAL (ALPHABETICS OR COMMON BREAK CHARACTERS) OR DUPLICATE EACH
OTHER.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##17	^^.IEPRV\\	^USER NOT PRIVILEGED TO PERFORM THE
SPECIFIED OPERATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##18	^^.IENAI\\	^DEVICE GIVEN AS AN ^^IMP\\ IN THE
PARAMETER BLOCK IS NOT REALLY AN ^^IMP\\.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##19	^^.IENNU\\	^NETWORK IS NOT UP.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.5 ^&^STATUS ^BITS AND ^STATE CODES\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FOLLOWING STATUS BITS AND CONNECTION STATE CODES ARE RETURNED
IN THE RIGHT HALF OF THE ^^.IBSTT\\ WORD BY THE
^^.IUSTT\\ FUNCTION, AND IN THE ^^.XSIST\\ AND ^^.XSOST\\ WORDS
BY THE ^^.IUXIS\\ FUNCTION:
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 1
.NOFILL
.TAB STOPS 8,16,25
^&^CODE	^SYMBOL	^MNEMONIC	^MEANING\&
.SKIP 1
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 25
.INDENT -25
^BIT#18	^^IR.RFM\\		^CONNECTION WAITING FOR A
^^RFNM\\ (^READY ^FOR ^NEXT ^MESSAGE) REPLY BEFORE SENDING
ANOTHER MESSAGE (TRANSMIT CONNECTIONS ONLY).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
30-35	^^IR.STT\\		^CONNECTION STATE, GIVEN AS
ONE OF THE FOLLOWING CODES:
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##0	^^.ISCLS	CLOSED\\	^CONNECTION IS CLOSED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##1	^^.ISLSN	LISTEN\\	^SOCKET IN THE LISTENING
STATE (ABLE TO ACCEPT INCOMING REQUESTS FOR CONNECTION).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##2	^^.ISRFN	RFCIN\\	^A REQUEST FOR CONNECTION HAS
BEEN RECEIVED ON A LISTENING SOCKET.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##3	^^.ISABT	ABORT\\	^A ^^CLS\\ (CLOSE) WAS RECEIVED FOR
A SOCKET IN THE ^^RFCIN\\ STATE (I.E., THE FOREIGN HOST IS
CANCELLING A PREVIOUS REQUEST FOR CONNECTION).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##4	^^.ISRFW	RFCOUT\\	^A REQUEST FOR CONNECTION
HAS BEEN SENT, BUT THE FOREIGN HOST HAS NOT YET EITHER ACKNOWLEDGED
OR REFUSED IT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##5	^^.ISOPN	OPEN\\	^THE CONNECTION IS OPEN AND CAN
PERFORM DATA TRANSFER.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##6	^^.ISCLW	CLSOUT\\	^A ^^CLS\\ (CLOSE REQUEST
VIA A ^^.IUCLS\\ FUNCTION) HAS BEEN SENT BUT NOT YET ACKNOWLEDGED
BY THE FOREIGN HOST.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##7	^^.ISRMW	RFMOUT\\	^WAITING FOR A ^^RFNM\\
BEFORE ACKNOWLEDGING A ^^CLS\\ RECEIVED FROM THE FOREIGN HOST.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##8	^^.ISIRW	ICPRFM\\	^^RFNM\\ WAIT IN ^^ICP\\
(^INITIAL ^CONNECTION ^PROTOCOL).  ^SOCKETS ESTABLISHED BY
USER PROGRAMS CANNOT GET INTO THIS STATE.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -25
##9	^^.ISCLD	CLSIN\\	^THE (INPUT) SOCKET HAS BEEN CLOSED,
BUT THERE IS STILL BUFFERED DATA TO BE READ FROM IT.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.6 ^&^OFFSETS FOR ^^.IUXIS\\ (^EXTENDED ^STATUS) ^FUNCTION\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^^.IUXIS\\ FUNCTION RETURNS EXTENDED STATUS FOR BOTH CONNECTIONS
ASSOCIATED WITH A SPECIFIED ^^IMP\\ DEVICE IN A VARIABLE LENGTH
PARAMETER BLOCK WHOSE LENGTH IS SPECIFIED BY THE USER PROGRAM.
^THUS, MORE WORDS OF STATUS INFORMATION MAY BE MADE AVAILABLE IN THE
FUTURE WITHOUT REQUIRING CHANGES TO EXISTING PROGRAMS (UNLESS
THEY WANT TO MAKE USE OF SUCH NEW INFORMATION).
.SKIP 1
.NOFILL
.TAB STOPS 8,16
^&^OFFSET	^SYMBOL	^CONTENTS\&
.SKIP 1
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.INDENT -16
##0	^^.XSNUM\\	^NUMBER OF WORDS OF STATUS INFORMATION
DESIRED (I.E., COUNT OF THE NUMBER OF WORDS THAT FOLLOW IN THE
PARAMETER BLOCK).  ^IF THIS WORD CONTAINS ZERO, IT IS DEFAULTED TO 9.
^IF MORE WORDS OF STATUS ARE REQUESTED THAN ARE ACTUALLY AVAILABLE,
THE REMAINING WORDS IN THE PARAMETER BLOCK ARE ZEROED.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##1	^^.XSDEV\\	^THE PHYSICAL OR LOGICAL ^^IMP\\ DEVICE NAME
FOR WHICH EXTENDED STATUS IS TO BE RETURNED.  ^^.XSNUM\\ AND
^^.XSDEV\\ ARE THE ONLY PARAMETERS WHICH NEED BE SUPPLIED
BY THE USER PROGRAM.  ^THE ^^.IUXIS\\ FUNCTION ALWAYS RETURNS
THE LOGICAL DEVICE NAME IN ^^.XSDEV\\ IF THERE IS ONE, ELSE THE
PHYSICAL NAME.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##2	^^.XSIST\\	^THE STATUS BITS AND STATE OF THE INPUT
SOCKET (SEE ^^.IBSTT\\).  ^ERROR CODES ARE ALSO RETURNED
IN THIS WORD.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##3	^^.XSILS\\	^LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER FOR THE INPUT
CONNECTION (SEE ^^.IBLCL)\\.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##4	^^.XSIHS\\	^HOST NUMBER FOR THE INPUT
CONNECTION (SEE ^^.IBHST\\).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##5	^^.XSIRS\\	^REMOTE SOCKET NUMBER FOR THE INPUT CONNECTION
(SEE ^^.IBRMT\\).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##6	^^.XSIBY\\	^THE BYTE SIZE FOR THE INPUT CONNECTION IN
THE LEFT HALF OF THE WORD.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##7	^^.XSOST\\	^THE STATUS BITS AND STATE OF THE OUTPUT
SOCKET (SEE ^^.IBSTT\\).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##8	^^.XSOLS\\	^LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER FOR THE OUTPUT
CONNECTION (SEE ^^.IBLCL\\).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##9	^^.XSOHS\\	^HOST NUMBER FOR THE OUTPUT
CONNECTION (SEE ^^.IBHST\\).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##10	^^.XSORS\\	^REMOTE SOCKET NUMBER FOR THE OUTPUT
CONNECTION (SEE ^^.IBRMT\\).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##11	^^.XSOBY\\	^THE BYTE SIZE FOR THE OUTPUT CONNECTION
IN THE LEFT HALF OF THE WORD.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##12	^^.XSIBA\\	^INPUT BIT ALLOCATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##13	^^.XSIMA\\	^INPUT MESSAGE ALLOCATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##14	^^.XSOBA\\	^OUTPUT BIT ALLOCATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##15	^^.XSOMA\\	^OUTPUT MESSAGE ALLOCATION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##16	^^.XSILK\\	^LINK NUMBER FOR THE INPUT CONNECTION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##17	^^.XSOLK\\	^LINK NUMBER FOR THE OUTPUT CONNECTION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##18	^^.XSIOS\\	^I/^O STATUS BITS FOR THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SKIP 1
^THE SYMBOL ^^.XSSIZ\\ (VALUE 19) DENOTES THE PRESENT SIZE OF
THE EXTENDED STATUS PARAMETER BLOCK.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.1.7 ^&^LOCAL ^SOCKET ^NUMBER ^CONVENTIONS\&
.PARAGRAPH
^LOCAL SOCKET NUMBERS, WHICH HAVE NO NETWORK-WIDE SIGNIFICANCE
EXCEPT FOR THE LOW-ORDER BIT, ARE ASSIGNED BY THE ^^NCP\\ IN
A MANNER DESIGNED TO MINIMIZE CONFLICTS BETWEEN PROGRAMS AND YET
ALLOW CONSIDERABLE FLEXIBILITY IN COMMUNICATION REQUIREMENTS.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN THE ^^DEC\\SYSTEM-10 IMPLEMENTATION, LOCAL SOCKET NUMBERS
ARE DIVIDED INTO RANGES OF 256 SOCKETS EACH.  ^THE ^^NCP\\
ALLOCATES THESE RANGES AS FOLLOWS, WITH SOCKET NUMBERS GIVEN
IN OCTAL:
.SKIP 1
.TAB STOPS 14
.LEFT MARGIN 14
.INDENT -14
00000000XXX	^FOR ALL XXX FROM 000 TO 777, RESERVED TO THE ^^NCP\\
FOR THE ^INITIAL ^CONNECTION ^PROTOCOL AND OTHER PURPOSES.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -14
00UUUUUUNXX	^WHERE NXX IS FROM 000 TO 377, ALLOCATED TO THE USER
WHOSE PROGRAMMER NUMBER IS UUUUUU.  ^A LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER IN
THIS FORM IS GENERATED WHEN A USER SPECIFIES A SOCKET NUMBER WHOSE
RIGHTMOST NINE BITS ARE FROM 000 THROUGH 377, MAKING IT A
USER-RELATIVE SOCKET NUMBER.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -14
00000JJJNXX	^WHERE NXX IS FROM 400 TO 777, ALLOCATED TO JOB
NUMBER JJJ.  ^A LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER IN THIS FORM IS GENERATED
WHEN A USER SPECIFIES A SOCKET NUMBER WHOSE RIGHTMOST NINE BITS
ARE FROM 400 THROUGH 777, MAKING IT A JOB-RELATIVE SOCKET NUMBER.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -14
00001KKKNXX	^WHERE NXX IS FROM 400 TO 777, ALLOCATED BY THE ^^NCP\\
WHEN A NEGATIVE LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER IS SPECIFIED BY A PROGRAM,
IN SUCH A WAY THAT SOCKETS 00001KKK400 THROUGH 00001KKK777 ARE
GUARANTEED TO BE UNUSED.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^BY SETTING THE ^^IF.ALS\\ BIT IN THE ^^IMPUUO\\ CONTROL WORD,
THE PROGRAM MAY SPECIFY AN ABSOLUTE, 32-BIT LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER.
^HOWEVER, WHETHER OR NOT THE PROGRAM IS PERMITTED TO USE THAT
SOCKET DEPENDS ON SEVERAL CONDITIONS.  ^IF THE ^^IF.PRV\\ BIT IS
ALSO SET AND THE PROGRAM IS PRIVILEGED, THEN ALL LOCAL SOCKETS IN THE
SYSTEM ARE AVAILABLE TO IT.  ^OTHERWISE, ACCESS IS PERMITTED TO
A LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER ONLY IF THE JOB ALREADY OWNS ONE OR MORE
SOCKETS IN THE SAME 256-WORD RANGE.
.PARAGRAPH
^IF A SPECIFIED LOCAL SOCKET NUMBER IS IN USE OR IN A RANGE NOT
AVAILABLE TO THE PROGRAM, THE ^^.IESKT\\ ERROR CODE IS RETURNED.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.2 ^&^I/^O ^PROGRAMMING\&
.PARAGRAPH
^AS PREVIOUSLY NOTED, NORMAL, DEVICE-INDEPENDENT ^I/^O MAY BE
PERFORMED VIA ^^IMP\\ DEVICES SUPPORTING OPEN CONNECTIONS.  ^THERE
ARE, HOWEVER, A NUMBER OF IMPORTANT PROGRAMMING CONSIDERATIONS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THERE ARE TWO DEVICE-DEPENDENT STATUS BITS, WHICH MAY BE TESTED
WITH THE ^^GETSTS\\, ^^STATZ\\, AND ^^STATO\\ ^^UUO\\S.
^THESE ARE:
.NOFILL
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 1
.TAB STOPS 8,16
^&^BIT	^SYMBOL	^MEANING\&
.SKIP 1
.FILL
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.INDENT -16
#25	^^IO.DAT\\	^INPUT DATA IS AVAILABLE FOR READING VIA
^^IN\\ OR ^^INPUT UUO\\S.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
#26	^^IO.NRM\\	^NOT READY FOR NEXT MESSAGE ON OUTPUT
CONNECTION (BECAUSE A ^^RFNM\\ IS OUTSTANDING).
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
#27	^^IO.INS\\	^AN ^^INS\\ (INTERRUPT BY SENDER) HAS BEEN
RECEIVED FOR THE INPUT CONNECTION.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
#28	^^IO.INR\\	^AN ^^INR\\ (INTERRUPT BY RECEIVER) HAS
BEEN RECEIVED FOR THE OUTPUT CONNECTION.
.SKIP 1
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^WHEN AN ^^IN\\ OR ^^INPUT UUO\\ IS EXECUTED, THE JOB IS
PUT INTO ^I/^O WAIT, IF NECESSARY, UNTIL AT LEAST ONE BYTE OF
DATA HAS ARRIVED FROM THE NETWORK OVER THAT CONNECTION.  ^THEN
ALL DATA THAT HAS BEEN RECEIVED IS PLACED INTO THE USER'S
BUFFER (PACKED ACCORDING TO THE DATA MODE--SEE BELOW), AND
CONTROL IS RETURNED TO THE USER PROGRAM.  ^HENCE, ^^IMP\\ INPUT
BUFFERS ARE GENERALLY NOT EXACTLY FILLED (HOWEVER, THE BYTE COUNT
SUPPLIED BY THE MONITOR IS ALWAYS CORRECT).
.PARAGRAPH
^IF A RECEIVE CONNECTION IS CLOSED BY THE FOREIGN HOST, THE NORMAL
END-OF-FILE FLAG (^^IO.EOF\\) WILL BE SET AFTER THE USER PROGRAM
HAS READ ALL BUFFERED DATA FROM THE CONNECTION.  ^BETWEEN THE TIME
THE CONNECTION IS CLOSED AND THE LAST DATA IS TAKEN BY THE USER PROGRAM,
THE CONNECTION WILL BE IN THE ^^CLSIN\\ STATE RATHER THAN THE
NORMAL ^^OPEN\\ STATE.
^IF THE FOREIGN HOST CRASHES DURING DATA TRANSFER, THE ^^IO.IMP\\
ERROR BIT WILL BE SET INSTEAD OF THE ^^IO.EOF\\ BIT.
.PARAGRAPH
^WHEN AN ^^OUT\\ OR ^^OUTPUT UUO\\ IS EXECUTED, THE JOB IS PUT
INTO ^I/^O WAIT, IF NECESSARY, UNTIL NO ^^RFNM\\ IS PENDING
FOR THE CONNECTION AND SUFFICIENT ALLOCATION HAS BEEN RECEIVED
TO TRANSMIT ALL THE DATA IN THE USER BUFFER.  ^AT THIS POINT,
ALL THE DATA IS PLACED IN INTERNAL MONITOR BUFFERS AND QUEUED
FOR OUTPUT TO THE FOREIGN HOST, AND CONTROL RETURNS
IMMEDIATELY TO THE USER PROGRAM.
.PARAGRAPH
^IF A SEND CONNECTION IS CLOSED BY THE FOREIGN HOST, OR THE FOREIGN
HOST GOES DOWN DURING DATA TRANSFER, THE ^^IO.IMP\\ ERROR BIT
WILL BE SET.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE FOLLOWING DATA MODES ARE SUPPORTED:  0 (^^ASCII\\), 1 (^^ASCII\\
LINE), 10 (IMAGE), 13 (IMAGE BINARY), AND 14 (BINARY).
^AT PRESENT, DATA MODES 0 AND 1 WORK CORRECTLY ONLY FOR CONNECTIONS
OPENED WITH A BYTE SIZE OF 8.  ^^ASCII\\ CODES 0 THROUGH 127 ARE
PACKED INTO THE USER BUFFER ON INPUT, WHILE CODES 128 THROUGH 255
(THE ^TELNET CONTROL CODES) ARE FLUSHED.  ^ON OUTPUT, 7-BIT ^^ASCII\\
CHARACTERS ARE SENT RIGHT-JUSTIFIED IN 8-BIT BYTES.  ^SIMILARLY,
DATA MODES 10, 13, AND 14 (WHICH ARE IDENTICAL TO EACH OTHER) WORK
PROPERLY ONLY FOR CONNECTIONS OPENED WITH A BYTE SIZE OF 36.
.PARAGRAPH
^TWO OTHER DATA MODES ARE SUPPORTED:  2 (8-BIT BYTE), AND 3 (8-BIT
BYTE WITH BREAK AFTER ANY MULTIPLE OF FOUR BYTES).  ^MODE 2 IS
USED TO TRANSMIT AND RECEIVE DATA OVER CONNECTIONS OPENED WITH
A BYTE SIZE OF 8 (OR ANY MULTIPLE OF 8), WITH THE ABILITY TO PROCESS
ALL 256 POSSIBLE CODES.  ^MODE 3 IS ALSO AN 8-BIT BYTE MODE, BUT
IS DESIGNED PRIMARILY FOR 32-BIT CONNECTIONS;  DATA WILL BE SENT
OR RECEIVED IN UNITS OF 32 BITS.
.PARAGRAPH
^THOUGH THE ^^NCP\\ ALLOWS CONNECTIONS TO BE OPENED USING ANY
BYTE SIZE, THE ^^IMP\\ DATA TRANSFER LOGIC CORRECTLY HANDLES
DATA ONLY OVER CONNECTIONS WHOSE BYTE SIZES ARE A MULTIPLE OF 8
OR 36.  ^SPECIAL USER CODING MAY BE EMPLOYED TO PERFORM ^I/^O TO
CONNECTIONS OF OTHER BYTE SIZES;  SUCH CODE SHOULD ^^OPEN\\ THE
^^IMP\\ DEVICE USING A 36-BIT DATA MODE (10-14) AND PACK OR UNPACK
THE DATA AS A STREAM OF CONTIGUOUS BYTES (IGNORING WORD BOUNDARIES).
^UNFORTUNATELY, IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO ACCURATELY DETERMINE THE
EXACT NUMBER OF BYTES IN A BUFFER WHEN DATA IS HANDLED IN THIS MANNER,
AND THIS METHOD WILL THEREFORE NOT BE USABLE IN SOME APPLICATIONS.
.PARAGRAPH
^A FUTURE REVISION OF THE ^^IMP\\ SERVICE DATA TRANSFER LOGIC
WILL PROBABLY ELIMINATE THE ABOVE-MENTIONED DEFICIENCIES, AS WELL
AS MAKING MODE 3 BECOME A TRUE 32-BIT DATA MODE.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.3 ^&^^IMP\\ ^SYSTEM ^^GETTAB\\S\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^^NCP\\ AND ^^IMP\\ DATA TRANSFER ROUTINES MAINTAIN A CONSIDERABLE
NUMBER OF OPERATING AND ERROR STATISTICS.  ^THESE ARE AVAILABLE
VIA THE ^^IMP\\ SYSTEM ^^GETTAB\\ TABLE (TABLE ^^.GTIMP\\,
VALUE -1).  ^THE DATA IS ORGANIZED INTO ^^GETTAB\\ SUBTABLES.
^THE FIRST FEW ENTRIES IN THE ^^.GTIMP\\ TABLE (AND THE ONLY
ONES WITH FIXED POSITIONS IN THE TABLE) ARE SUBTABLE POINTERS.
^TO OBTAIN AN ENTRY FROM A PARTICULAR TABLE, THE USER PROGRAM MUST
FIRST OBTAIN THE SUBTABLE POINTER FOR THE TABLE, THEN ADD
THE DESIRED INDEX TO THE SUBTABLE POINTER AND PERFORM ANOTHER ^^GETTAB\\
TO READ THE ACTUAL ENTRY.  ^FOR EXAMPLE, TO OBTAIN THE NUMBER OF
FREE ^^IMP\\ BUFFERS (ENTRY ^^%ISNFB\\ OF TABLE ^^%ISBHS\\), THE
FOLLOWING ^^MACRO-10\\ CODE MIGHT BE USED:
.NOFILL
.TEST PAGE 9
.SKIP 1
.TAB STOPS 8,16,24,32,40
^^	MOVE	AC,[%ISBHS]	;GET IMP BUFFER HANDLING
	GETTAB	AC,		;  STATISTICS SUBTABLE
	  JRST	ERROR		;  POINTER.
	MOVSI	AC,%ISNFB(AC)	;ADD SUBTABLE INDEX.
	HRRI	AC,.GTIMP	;NOW GET THE ACTUAL DATA
	GETTAB	AC,		;  FROM THE SUBTABLE.
	  JRST	ERROR
; AC NOW CONTAINS THE DESIRED DATA\\
.FILL
.SKIP 1
.PARAGRAPH
^A LISTING OF ^^IMP.MAC\\ SHOULD BE CONSULTED FOR INFORMATION
ON THE CURRENT CONTENTS OF THE ^^IMP\\ SYSTEM ^^GETTAB\\ TABLE.
^THE ^^IMPCOM\\ PROGRAM PRINTS ALL AVAILABLE STATISTICS VIA
THE '^^IMP#ERROR\\' COMMAND.
.TEST PAGE 6
.SKIP 2
2.4 ^&^MISCELLANEOUS\&
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^^IMP\\ DEVICE TYPE CODE (^^TY.DEV\\ FIELD RETURNED BY THE
^^DEVTYP UUO\\) IS 77 (OCTAL).
.PAGE
.CENTER; ^APPENDIX
.SKIP 2
^OLD ^FORMAT ^^UUO\\
.PARAGRAPH
^BECAUSE OF AN EXPANDED ^HOST-^^IMP\\ LEADER FORMAT IN
THE ^NETWORK, THE NUMBER OF BITS WHICH ARE USED TO
DESIGNATE A PARTICULAR ^HOST GREW FROM 8 BITS TO 32 BITS.
^IN ORDER TO CREATE THE SPACE NEEDED TO DELIVER THESE ADDITIONAL
BITS TO THE USER, THE ^^UUO\\ BLOCK HAD TO BE CHANGED.
^ALL PROGRAMS BEING WRITTEN SHOULD SET THE ^^IF.NEW\\ BIT IN
THE ^^UUO\\'S CONTROL WORD AND USE THE STANDARD PARAMETER BLOCK
DESCRIBED IN ^SECTION 2.1.2.  ^WE ALSO ENCOURAGE THAT OLD PROGRAMS
BE MODIFIED TO USE THE NEW FORMAT.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN ORDER TO MAKE THE CONVERSION OF OLD PROGRAMS POSSIBLE,
WE WILL NOW DESCRIBE THE OLD ^^UUO\\ FORMAT.
^TO BEGIN WITH, THE 8 BIT ^HOST NUMBER OF THE OLD FORMAT IS NOW A
32 BIT ^SITE NUMBER.  ^THE NEW FORMAT, AS DESCRIBED IN ^SECTION 2.1.2,
HAS THREE DISTINCT NUMBERS CONTAINED IN ITS 32 BITS.  ^THESE
THREE NUMBERS RELATE TO THE 8 BITS OF THE HOST NUMBER OF THE OLD FORMAT
AS FOLLOWS:
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
^NETWORK NUMBER	^THERE IS NO ^NETWORK NUMBER IN THE OLD FORMAT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
^HOST NUMBER	^THE HIGH ORDER TWO BITS OF THE OLD FORMAT ^HOST
NUMBER CORRESPONDS TO THE ^HOST NUMBER OF THE NEW FORMAT.  ^NOTE
THAT ALTHOUGH THERE MAY BE ^HOST NUMBERS AS HIGH AS 255, ONLY THOSE
^SITES WITH ^HOST NUMBERS LESS THAN 4 CAN BE ADDRESSED USING THE
OLD ^^UUO\\ FORMAT.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
^^IMP\\ NUMBER	^THE LOW ORDER 6 BITS OF THE OLD FORMAT ^HOST NUMBER
CORRESPOND TO THE ^^IMP\\ NUMBER OF THE NEW FORMAT.  ^ONLY ^SITES
WITH ^^IMP\\ NUMBERS LESS THAN 64 CAN BE ADDRESSED USING THE OLD
FORMAT ^^UUO\\.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.PARAGRAPH
^THE OLD FORMAT OF THE ^^IMP\\ ^^UUO\\ HAS A SLIGHTLY DIFFERENT
PARAMETER BLOCK THAN THE NEW FORMAT.
^THE OLD FORMAT BLOCK IS AS FOLLOWS:
.SKIP 1
.NOFILL
^&^OFFSET	^SYMBOL	^COMMENTS\&
.LEFT MARGIN 16
.SKIP 1
.FILL
.INDENT -16
##0	^^.IBDEV\\	SAME AS IN NEW FORMAT
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##1	^^.IBSST\\	SAME AS IN NEW FORMAT
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##2	^^.IBLCL\\	SAME AS IN NEW FORMAT
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##3	^^.IBHST\\	^THE ^HOST NUMBER, A NUMBER BETWEEN
1 AND 255, IS IN THE RIGHT HALF OF THE WORD.
.INDENT -16
	^^.IBBYT\\	^THE CONNECTION BYTE SIZE IS IN THE LEFT HALF
OF THE ^^.IBHST\\ WORD.
.SKIP 1
.INDENT -16
##4	^^.IBRMT\\	^SAME AS IN NEW FORMAT.
.LEFT MARGIN 0
.SKIP 1
^^.IBSIZ\\ FOR THE OLD FORMAT WAS EQUAL TO 5.  ^ALL OTHER ASPECTS
OF THE STANDARD BLOCK ARE THE SAME FOR BOTH FORMATS.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN THE OLD FORMAT, TWO OF THE ^^UUO\\ FUNCTIONS
RETURN VALUES IN DIFFERENT WORDS
THAN THEY DO IN THE NEW FORMAT.
^THE TWO FUNCTIONS ARE ^^.IULHS\\, THE FUNCTION WHICH RETURNS
INFORMATION ABOUT THE LOCAL HOST, AND ^^.IUXIS\\, THE EXTENDED
STATUS FUNCTION DESCRIBED IN ^SECTION 2.1.6.  ^THE DIFFERENCES
ARE DESCRIBED BELOW.
.PARAGRAPH
^IN THE OLD FORMAT, ALL OF THE INFORMATION RETURNED BY THE ^^.IULHS\\
FUNCTION IS RETURNED IN THE ^^.IBHST\\ WORD.  ^THE LOCAL HOST NUMBER,
A NUMBER BETWEEN 1 AND 155, IS RETURNED IN THE RIGHT HALF OF THE
WORD.  ^THE NUMBER OF NETWORK TERMINALS AND ^^IMP\\ DEVICES
ARE IN BITS 1-8 AND 9-17 RESPECTIVELY.  ^BIT 0 INDICATES THAT THE
^NETWORK IS AVAILABLE.
.PARAGRAPH
^THE ^^.XSIBY\\ AND ^^.XSOBY\\ WORDS OF THE ^^.IUXIS\\ FUNCTION
DO NOT EXIST IN THE OLD FORMAT.  ^THE ^^.XSIHS\\ AND ^^.XSOHS\\
WORDS CONTAIN THE BYTE SIZE OF THE CONNECTION IN EXACTLY THE SAME
MANNER AS IN THE ^^.IBHST\\ WORD OF THE OLD FORMAT DATA BLOCK AS
DESCRIBED ABOVE.  ^ALL OF THE OTHER SYMBOLS ARE THE SAME AS IN THE
NEW FORMAT, ALTHOUGH SOME OF THEIR VALUES ARE 1 OR 2 LESS BECAUSE
OF THE ABSENCE OF THE ^^.XSIBY\\ AND ^^.XSOBY\\ WORDS.
